{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And their eyes were opened, etc.</b> According to what we explained in the verse (Genesis 2:25) ״וַיִּהְיוּ שְׁנֵיהֶם עֲרוּמִּים״ — that sin produces the awareness of nakedness, which in turn produces the shame of nakedness — this is what the verse means when it says ״וַתִּפָּקַחְנָה״ etc., and each one was ashamed before the other. Further, it may hint in saying <b>וַתִּפָּקַחְנָה</b> in the manner of the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Sotah 3a): a person does not commit a transgression unless a spirit of folly — i.e., the dimming — enters him; and this is what Scripture attributes to the wicked, as it says (Isaiah 42:18) ״וְהַעִוְרִים הַבִּיטוּ לִרְאוֹת״, and this is what Scripture informs us here in saying ״וַתִּפָּקַחְנָה״ — meaning that after they committed the transgression, and the tempter — who is characterized by the attribute of darkness, which darkens a person's eyes — had passed, immediately their eyes were opened and they realized that they were naked, meaning that they had been stripped of the radiance of holiness. And so it will happen to every wicked person: upon completing the evil deed he will realize that he has acted corruptly, and he will be ashamed of his actions, and the eyes of his understanding will be illuminated. And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 14), who said in this language: the garment of the first Adam was a skin of fingernail and a cloud of glory covering him — the verse is properly understood in saying <b>כִּי עֵרֻמִּים הֵם</b>, meaning naked of the garment that had been upon them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:44.176784580Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}